From 83f066ffe16fd72c0a1b0716ab63390002c10750 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: radxa <radxa@radxa-cubie-a7a>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 22:17:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] gallium/pvr: submit render passes in batches and own the
 pending streams

Renders are now recorded in a pending queue and submitted up to eight per
DRM_IOCTL_PVR_SUBMIT_JOBS instead of one ioctl per render, with eight draw
slots and a second HWRT data set per target size so the CPU can run ahead of
the GPU.  The kernel copies each command stream through a user pointer
(memdup_user) at submit time, so a pending render must own its stream bytes:
they are copied into the pending entry instead of pointing at pvr_draw_flush's
stack buffers (submitting stale stack bytes made the kernel reject the ioctl
with EINVAL and, with more than one render, oops in the job setup).

A NO_DEFER render (a materialized clear and other renders a reader waits for)
is submitted at once, and pvr_draw_resource_sync() submits the pending batch
before it looks for completion syncs, so CPU readers never observe an
unsubmitted render.

No measurable speedup on the passes microbenchmark yet: the cost is one GPU
kick per render pass (0.26 ms) rather than the ioctl round trip, which this
removes.  It is the infrastructure the pass-merging change needs.

17-group gate: ALL GROUPS PASS.
